$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Step-I (olefination): To a solution of building block 4 (0.1g, 0.2 mmol)
in dry DMF at 0°C, under nitrogen atmosphere was added sodium hydride
(0.012g, 0.3 mmol). The solution turned reddish orange indicating the
formation of carbanion. After 10 min of stirring, a solution of aldehydes
13a-m (0.24 mmol, 1.2 eq) in DMF (1 mL) was added. The temperature of the
reaction was gradually raised from 0°C to room temperature and stirring
continued at room temperature for 4-5 h. The progress of the reaction was
monitored by TLC and after complete consumption of starting material; the
reaction was quenched with ice cold water (15 mL) and extracted with
ethyl acetate (2×15 mL). The combined organic extracts were washed with
water (20 mL), brine (20 mL) and dried over anhydrous sodium sulphate,
filtered, and the filtrate was concentrated under vacuum. The crude
compound was subjected to column chromatography using silica gel as
stationary phase, and combination of hexane/ethyl acetate as mobile phase
gave the protected olefinated products 14a-m. (E)-Methyl
2-acetoxy-4-methoxy-3-(3-methylbut-2-en-1-yl)-6-styrylbenzoate (14a)
Yield = 0.078g, (83%). R f = 0.4 (EtOAc-Hexanes 1:9); white solid mp =
61-62°C; IR (KBr Pellet); 2924, 2853, 1762, 1710, 1636, 1605, 1558, 1207,
1048 cm-1; 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ: 7.47 (d, J = 7.2 Hz, 1H, ArH);
7.37-7.31 (m, 4H, ArH, CH); 7.28 (d, J = 7.2 Hz, 1H, ArH); 7.01 (s, 1H,
ArH); 6.96 (d, J = 16.0 Hz, 1H, CH); 5.10 (t, J = 6.8 Hz, 1H, CH); 3.92
(s, 3H, OCH3); 3.87 (s, 3H, OCH3); 3.23 (d, J = 6.8 Hz, 2H, CH2); 2.30
(s, 3H, COCH3); 1.73 (s, 3H, CH3); 1.66 (s, 3H, CH3) ppm. 13C NMR (100
MHz, CDCl3) δ: 169.1 (C); 166.9 (C); 159.7 (C); 148.3 (C); 137.2 (C);
137.0 (C); 132.2 (C); 131.5 (C); 128.8 (CH); 128.1 (CH); 126.9 (CH);
126.6 (CH); 123.4 (C); 121.3 (CH); 118.0 (C); 105.8 (CH); 56.0 (OCH3);
52.3 (OCH3); 25.8 (CH3); 23.6 (CH2); 20.8 (CH3); 17.9 (CH3) ppm.
HRMS-ESI: Calcd. For C24H27O5 [M+H]+: 395.1858, 395.1867.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective (E), Julia olefination
